### What is a Flink checkpoint?

A checkpoint is triggered automatically by the checkpoint coordinator on the interval you configure. It is owned and managed by Flink, which decides when to create and delete it. Checkpoints prioritize low overhead.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
    StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

// Automatic checkpoints every 10 seconds
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointStorage(
    "s3://nbi-flink-checkpoints/");

// Checkpoints are managed by Flink:
// - Created automatically on interval
// - Old ones deleted when newer completes
// - May be incremental (RocksDB)
```

### What is a Flink savepoint?

A savepoint is triggered manually by an operator, stored in a portable format, and persists until explicitly deleted. It is designed for planned lifecycle operations.

```bash
# Take a savepoint
flink savepoint <jobId> s3://nbi-savepoints/fraud-scoring-v2

# Stop with final savepoint (graceful)
flink stop --savepoint s3://nbi-savepoints/pre-upgrade <jobId>

# Restore from savepoint
flink run -s s3://nbi-savepoints/pre-upgrade \
    --allowNonRestoredState \
    fraud-scoring-v3.jar
```

### How are they different?

| Aspect | Checkpoint | Savepoint |
|---|---|---|
| Trigger | Automatic (periodic) | Manual (user-initiated) |
| Owned by | Flink | Operations team |
| Purpose | Crash recovery | Upgrades, rescaling, migration |
| Format | May be backend-specific, incremental | Canonical, portable, self-contained |
| Lifecycle | Deleted when superseded or job stops | Persists until explicitly deleted |
| Portability | Not guaranteed across versions | Designed for cross-version restore |
| Speed | Optimized for low overhead | May be heavier (full format) |

```java
// Checkpoint: you never think about it
// Job crashes → Flink restores from latest checkpoint automatically

// Savepoint: deliberate operation
// Deploy new version → take savepoint → stop old → start new from savepoint
```

### Why are operator UIDs essential for savepoints?

Savepoint restore matches state to operators by their assigned unique IDs. Without stable UIDs, a code change can orphan state and fail the restore.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;

// CORRECT -- stable UIDs on every stateful operator
DataStream<String> result = txnStream
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new BalanceTracker())
    .uid("balance-tracker")          // stable UID
    .name("Balance Tracker");

result
    .keyBy(alert -> alert.getAccountId())
    .process(new FraudScorer())
    .uid("fraud-scorer")             // stable UID
    .name("Fraud Scorer");

// WRONG -- no UIDs, savepoint restore breaks on code change
txnStream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new BalanceTracker());  // no uid()!
// If code changes reorder operators, state cannot be matched
```

### When do you use each?

Checkpoints are always on for crash recovery. Savepoints are for planned lifecycle events: code upgrades, parallelism changes, Flink version upgrades, state migration, and debugging.

```bash
# Scenario 1: Code upgrade
flink stop --savepoint s3://nbi-savepoints/v2-pre-upgrade <jobId>
flink run -s s3://nbi-savepoints/v2-pre-upgrade fraud-scoring-v3.jar

# Scenario 2: Rescale from 4 to 8 slots
flink stop --savepoint s3://nbi-savepoints/pre-rescale <jobId>
flink run -s s3://nbi-savepoints/pre-rescale -p 8 fraud-scoring.jar

# Scenario 3: Debug a production issue
flink savepoint <jobId> s3://nbi-savepoints/debug-snapshot
# Restore locally with small test data for debugging
```

### What about checkpoint retention?

By default checkpoints are deleted when the job stops. You can configure retention so a checkpoint survives cancellation for emergency recovery.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.CheckpointConfig;

// Retain checkpoint on cancellation
env.getCheckpointConfig().setExternalizedCheckpointCleanup(
    CheckpointConfig.ExternalizedCheckpointCleanup
        .RETAIN_ON_CANCELLATION);
// Now the last checkpoint survives job cancellation
// Useful for emergency recovery when savepoint was not taken
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not assigning stable UIDs to stateful operators, which makes savepoint restore fail on code changes. Another is relying solely on checkpoints for planned upgrades, which may not be portable.

```java
// WRONG -- no UIDs on stateful operators
// Savepoint taken, code refactored, restore fails!

// CORRECT -- always assign uid() to every stateful operator
stream.process(new BalanceTracker()).uid("balance-tracker");
stream.process(new FraudScorer()).uid("fraud-scorer");
// Savepoint restore matches state by UID, code changes are safe
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Test savepoint-restore as part of your deployment pipeline. Discovering a broken state migration in production during an upgrade is extremely costly for a bank. Assign stable UIDs from day one, take savepoints before every planned change, and retain at least one checkpoint on cancellation as a safety net.
